Approach: Use sliding window with HashMap to store the last index of each character. If a duplicate appears within the current window, move left pointer ahead and track maximum substring length.

Time Complexity: O(n) — each character is processed once with O(1) average HashMap operations.

Space Complexity: O(n) — HashMap stores up to n characters in the worst case.

class Solution {
    public int lengthOfLongestSubstring(String s) {
        int left=0,right=0,maxlen=0,n=s.length();
        Map<Character,Integer> map=new HashMap<>();
        while(right<n){
             if(map.containsKey(s.charAt(right)) && map.get(s.charAt(right))>=left){
                left=map.get(s.charAt(right))+1;
             }
             map.put(s.charAt(right),right);
             maxlen=Math.max(maxlen,right-left+1);
             right++;
        }
        return maxlen;
